{"cuisine/cuisine-of-the-southwestern-united-states":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-the-southwestern-united-states","n":"Southwestern American cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["The cuisine of the Southwestern United States is food styled after the cooking of the Oasisamerican Native Americans, Hispanos of New Mexico, caballero, cowboys, hillbillies, and Mexican Americans."],["One such style within the Southwest is New Mexican cuisine, named after the state of New Mexico and the Southwest's historical naming convention of Nuevo México. It is most popular in the states of New Mexico, Colorado, Arizona, Southern Nevada and Utah. This cuisine is known for its use of New Mexico chile; the majority of the crop is grown in Hatch, New Mexico. A hallmark of New Mexican cuisine is smothering dishes with either red chile, green chile, or both (a combination referred to as \"Christmas\"). Beyond chiles, it incorporates flavors such as piñon, and dishes such as breakfast burritos, biscochitos, and sopapillas. Another popular variety is the Tejano foods from Texan cuisine, called Tex-Mex, which includes dishes like fajitas and from Texan barbecue. Additionally, parts of Arizona’s Southwestern cuisine are often called Sonoran, named after the Mexican state of Sonora and the Sonoran Desert that covers a third of the state, and include dishes like Sonoran hot dogs."],["A number of casual dining and fast food restaurants specializing in Southwestern cuisine have become popular in the United States. Several brands specialize in Southwestern foods for grocers."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-the-southwestern-united-states"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_the_Southwestern_United_States"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Coyote Cafe","ref":"cookbook/coyote-cafe","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Mark Miller, 1989","note":"Southwestern recipes from the influential Santa Fe restaurant.","url":"","suggested":true},{"name":"Authentic Recipes from Santa Fe","ref":"cookbook/authentic-recipes-from-santa-fe","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Dave DeWitt and Nancy Gerlach","note":"Illustrated collection of Santa Fe and American Southwest dishes.","url":"https://tuttlepublishing.com/other/authentic-recipes-from-santa-fe-9780804859844","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Discada","dish/discada"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Southwestern American cooking","count":2,"links":[["New Mexican cuisine","cuisine/new-mexican-cuisine"],["Texan cuisine","cuisine/texan-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/0f/Pot-o-chili.jpg/500px-Pot-o-chili.jpg","w":500,"h":353,"by":"FiveRings at English Wikipedia","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pot-o-chili.jpg"}},"cuisine/korean-chinese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/korean-chinese-cuisine","n":"Korean Chinese cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Diaspora cuisine in South Korea","intro":[["Korean Chinese cuisine, also known as Sino–Korean cuisine, is a hybrid cuisine developed by the ethnic Chinese in Korea."],["Despite originally being derived from Chinese cuisine, Korean-Chinese cuisine consists of unique dishes with Korean flavours and ingredients, making it a hybrid cuisine."],["In South Korea, the food is usually delivered. In other parts of the world, Korean Chinese dishes are typically served in Korean restaurants as well as in Chinese restaurants whose owners are immigrants from Korea or if they are from a Chinese–Korean family."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"korean-chinese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Korean_Chinese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Korean Bapsang","ref":"site/koreanbapsang-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"koreanbapsang.com","note":"Korean Bapsang's section on Korean Chinese dishes such as jjajangmyeon and jjamppong.","url":"https://www.koreanbapsang.com/category/korean-chinese","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":5,"links":[["Hotteok","dish/hotteok"],["Jjajangmyeon","dish/jjajangmyeon"],["Jjamppong","dish/jjamppong"],["Jungguk-naengmyeon","dish/jungguk-naengmyeon"],["Ulmyeon","dish/ulmyeon"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Noodles","ingredient/noodles"],["Brown sugar","ingredient/brown-sugar"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Honey","ingredient/honey"],["Peanut","ingredient/peanut"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"],["Cumian","ingredient/cumian"],["Sweet bean sauce","ingredient/sweet-bean-sauce"],["Agaricus bisporus","ingredient/agaricus-bisporus"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Cornstarch","ingredient/cornstarch"],["Cuttlefish","ingredient/cuttlefish"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Noodle","ingredient/noodle"],["Sea cucumber","ingredient/sea-cucumber"],["Shiitake","ingredient/shiitake"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"],["Squid as food","ingredient/squid-as-food"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/15/Korean_Chinese_dumplings_and_Korean_Chinese_sweet_and_sour_pork.jpg/500px-Korean_Chinese_dumplings_and_Korean_Chinese_sweet_and_sour_pork.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"User:Piotrus","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korean_Chinese_dumplings_and_Korean_Chinese_sweet_and_sour_pork.jpg"}},"ingredient/goldfish":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/goldfish","n":"Goldfish","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["The goldfish (Carassius auratus) is a freshwater fish in the family Cyprinidae of the order Cypriniformes. It is commonly kept as a pet in indoor aquariums, and is one of the most popular aquarium fish. Goldfish released into the wild have become an invasive pest in parts of North America and Australia."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-goldfish"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldfish"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Arsik","dish/arsik"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Asparagus bean","ingredient/asparagus-bean"],["Bay leaf","ingredient/bay-leaf"],["Candlenut","ingredient/candlenut"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Chives","ingredient/chives"],["Etlingera elatior","ingredient/etlingera-elatior"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Garcinia atroviridis","ingredient/garcinia-atroviridis"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/65/Gold_fish1.jpg/500px-Gold_fish1.jpg","w":500,"h":326,"by":"לינה אבוגוש","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_fish1.jpg"}},"ingredient/menma":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/menma","n":"Menma","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Menma is the Japanese name for the Chinese dish sunsi, which are lacto-fermented bamboo shoots. The bamboo shoots are dried in the sun or through other means before the process of fermentation. Menma is a common topping for noodle soups, notably ramen. Menma is primarily produced in China, with brands imported from southern China and Taiwan being popular. Menma is also known as shinachiku (支那竹), \"Chinese bamboo\"."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-menma"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Menma"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Taiwan cuisine","cuisine/taiwan-cuisine",[["Braised pork rice","dish/braised-pork-rice"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/61/Tokyoramen.jpg/500px-Tokyoramen.jpg","w":500,"h":507,"by":"ja:User:Hykw-a4","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tokyoramen.jpg"}},"ingredient/succade":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/succade","n":"Succade","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Succade is the candied peel of any of the citrus species, especially from the citron or Citrus medica which is distinct with its extra-thick peel; in addition, the taste of the inner rind of the citron is less bitter than those of the other citrus. However, the term is also occasionally applied to the peel, root, or even entire fruit or vegetable like parsley, fennel and cucurbita which have a bitter taste and are boiled with sugar to get a special \"sweet and sour\" outcome."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-succade"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Succade"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Dutch cuisine","cuisine/dutch-cuisine",[["Oliebol","dish/oliebol"]]],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Cassatella di sant'Agata","dish/cassatella-di-sant-agata"]]],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Nesselrode pudding","dish/nesselrode-pudding"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Sponge cake","ingredient/sponge-cake"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Candied peel","ingredient/candied-peel"],["Ceramic glaze","ingredient/ceramic-glaze"],["Fruit juice","ingredient/fruit-juice"],["Icing","ingredient/icing"],["Liqueur","ingredient/liqueur"],["Marzipan","ingredient/marzipan"],["Ricotta","ingredient/ricotta"],["Currant","ingredient/currant"],["Egg yolk","ingredient/egg-yolk"],["Maraschino","ingredient/maraschino"],["Powdered sugar","ingredient/powdered-sugar"],["Whipped cream","ingredient/whipped-cream"],["Baking powder","ingredient/baking-powder"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/ba/Succade.jpg/500px-Succade.jpg","w":500,"h":400,"by":"Ich","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Succade.jpg"}},"ingredient/lentil":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/lentil","n":"Lentil","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 6 dishes across 6 cuisines","intro":[["The lentil (Vicia lens or Lens culinaris) is an annual legume grown for its lens-shaped edible seeds or pulses, also called lentils. It is about 40 cm (16 in) tall, and the seeds grow in pods, usually with two seeds in each."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-lentil"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lentil"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":6,"groups":[["Cypriot cuisine","cuisine/cypriot-cuisine",[["Mujaddara","dish/mujaddara"]]],["Gujarati cuisine","cuisine/gujarati-cuisine",[["Dhokla","dish/dhokla"]]],["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine",[["Hyderabadi haleem","dish/hyderabadi-haleem"]]],["Maharashtrian cuisine","cuisine/maharashtrian-cuisine",[["Usal","dish/usal"]]],["Moroccan cuisine","cuisine/moroccan-cuisine",[["Harira","dish/harira"]]],["Parsi cuisine","cuisine/parsi-cuisine",[["Dhansak","dish/dhansak"]]]]},{"h":"Where it's grown","grown":{"what":"Lentils, dry","years":"2020 to 2024","url":"https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/QCL","world":6930568,"top":[["Canada","cuisine/canadian-cuisine",2204874],["India","cuisine/indian-cuisine",1443156],["Ashmore and Cartier Is.","cuisine/australian-cuisine",1274473],["Turkey","cuisine/turkish-cuisine",391563],["United States of America","cuisine/american-cuisine",283622],["Nepal","cuisine/nepalese-cuisine",222987],["Russia","cuisine/russian-cuisine",194533],["Bangladesh","cuisine/bangladeshi-cuisine",187049],["Kazakhstan","cuisine/kazakh-cuisine",180273],["China","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",166683],["Ethiopia","cuisine/ethiopian-cuisine",111698],["Iran","cuisine/iranian-cuisine",77909],["Syria","cuisine/syrian-cuisine",77663],["Argentina","cuisine/argentine-cuisine",29906],["Morocco","cuisine/moroccan-cuisine",14518]]}},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Cumin seed","ingredient/cumin-seed"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Gourd","ingredient/gourd"],["Pumpkin","ingredient/pumpkin"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Mustard oil","ingredient/mustard-oil"],["Sodium bicarbonate","ingredient/sodium-bicarbonate"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Chickpea","ingredient/chickpea"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f5/3_types_of_lentil.png/500px-3_types_of_lentil.png","w":500,"h":287,"by":"User:Justinc","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:3_types_of_lentil.png"}},"ingredient/rectum":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/rectum","n":"Rectum","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["The rectum is the final straight portion of the large intestine in humans and some other mammals, and the gut in others. Before expulsion through the anus or cloaca, the rectum stores the feces temporarily. The adult human rectum is about 12 centimetres (4.7 in) long, and begins at the rectosigmoid junction (the end of the sigmoid colon) at the level of the third sacral vertebra or the sacral promontory depending upon what definition is used. Its diameter is similar to that of the sigmoid colon at its commencement, but it is dilated near its termination, forming the rectal ampulla. It terminates at the level of the anorectal ring (the level of the puborectalis sling) or the dentate line, again depending upon which definition is used. In humans, the rectum is followed by the anal canal, which is about 4 centimetres (1.6 in) long, before the gastrointestinal tract t"]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-rectum"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rectum"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine",[["Makchang","dish/makchang"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Abomasum","ingredient/abomasum"]]}]},"ingredient/carabao-mango":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/carabao-mango","n":"Carabao mango","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 3 cuisines","intro":[["The Carabao mango, also known as the Philippine mango or Manila mango among other names, is a variety of particularly sweet mango from the Philippines. One of the most important varieties cultivated in the Philippines, the carabao mango is known internationally for its sweetness and exotic taste. It is named after the carabao, a native Filipino breed of domesticated water buffalo considered the unofficial national animal."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-carabao-mango"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carabao_mango"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine",[["Mango float","dish/mango-float"]]],["Mindanao cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-mindanao",[["Mango float","dish/mango-float"]]],["Visayan cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-visayas",[["Mango float","dish/mango-float"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Condensed milk","ingredient/condensed-milk"],["Cracker","ingredient/cracker"],["Graham crackers","ingredient/graham-crackers"],["Ladyfinger (biscuit)","ingredient/ladyfinger-biscuit"],["Mango","ingredient/mango"],["Savoiarde PAT","ingredient/savoiarde-pat"],["Whipped cream","ingredient/whipped-cream"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/fb/Carabao_mangoes_%28Philippines%29.jpg/500px-Carabao_mangoes_%28Philippines%29.jpg","w":500,"h":458,"by":"Obsidian Soul","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carabao_mangoes_(Philippines).jpg"}},"ingredient/bakwan":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/bakwan","n":"Bakwan","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Bakwan are vegetable fritters or gorengan commonly found in Indonesia. Bakwan are usually sold by traveling street vendors. The ingredients are vegetables—usually bean sprouts, shredded cabbage and carrots—battered and deep-fried in cooking oil. To achieve a crispy texture, the batter uses a mixture of flour, corn starch  and sago or tapioca. In West Java bakwan is known as bala-bala and in Semarang is called badak. It is similar to Japanese yasai tenpura (vegetable tempura), Korean pajeon, Bruneian cucur, Indian pakora, Burmese a-kyaw, Caribbean pholourie or Filipino ukoy."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-bakwan"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bakwan"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Nasi tempong","dish/nasi-tempong"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Amaranth","ingredient/amaranth"],["Bird's eye chili","ingredient/bird-s-eye-chili"],["Cosmos","ingredient/cosmos"],["Holy basil","ingredient/holy-basil"],["Ikan goreng","ingredient/ikan-goreng"],["Peanut sauce","ingredient/peanut-sauce"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Tahu goreng","ingredient/tahu-goreng"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/79/Cabbage_fritters_gorengan.JPG/500px-Cabbage_fritters_gorengan.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Sakurai Midori","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cabbage_fritters_gorengan.JPG"}},"dish/paella":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/paella","n":"Paella","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Valencian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Paella is a rice dish originally from the Valencian Community. Paella is regarded as one of the community's identifying symbols."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-paella"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paella"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Valencian cuisine","cuisine/valencian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":9,"links":[["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Green bean","ingredient/green-bean"],["Lima bean","ingredient/lima-bean"],["Lima beans","ingredient/lima-beans"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Rabbit meat","ingredient/rabbit-meat"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Rice as food","ingredient/rice-as-food"],["Saffron","ingredient/saffron"]]},{"h":"More from Valencian cuisine","links":[["Arròs negre","dish/arros-negre"],["Esgarrat","dish/esgarrat"],["Puchero","dish/puchero"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d5/Cooking_a_paella.jpg/500px-Cooking_a_paella.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Jebulon","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cooking_a_paella.jpg"}},"dish/pasulj":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/pasulj","n":"Pasulj","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Serbian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Pasulj, grah (грах) or grav (грав) is a bean stew or soup made of usually white, cranberry, pinto or  kidney beans, which is a popular dish in Balkan cuisine. It is normally prepared with meat, particularly smoked meat such as smoked bacon, sausage, and ham hock, and is a dish typically eaten in the winter months, especially around the Christmas period. Other commonly used ingredients include carrots and onions. Another version of the dish using baked beans is known as prebranac. The dish can be served both hot or cold, and it is typically served with a side of sour cream or ajvar, and bread."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-pasulj"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pasulj"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Serbian cuisine","cuisine/serbian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Apium graveolens var. dulce","ingredient/apium-graveolens-var-dulce"],["Bean","ingredient/bean"],["Bell pepper","ingredient/bell-pepper"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Smoked meat","ingredient/smoked-meat"],["White bean","ingredient/white-bean"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Balkan Lunchbox: Kosovo recipes","ref":"site/balkanlunchbox-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"balkanlunchbox.com","note":"Balkan home cooking blog with a section of Serbian dishes.","url":"https://balkanlunchbox.com/category/country/serbian-recipes/page/3","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Serbian cuisine","links":[["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Karađorđeva šnicla","dish/kara-or-eva-snicla"],["Pindjur","dish/pindjur"],["Pljeskavica","dish/pljeskavica"],["Stuffed leaves","dish/stuffed-leaves"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/e2/Pasulj_%2810291319804%29.jpg/500px-Pasulj_%2810291319804%29.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Ivana Sokolović","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pasulj_(10291319804).jpg"}},"dish/qalayet-bandora":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/qalayet-bandora","n":"Qalayet bandora","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 3 cuisines","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Qalayet bandora is a simple Levantine dish of tomatoes, onions, hot peppers (usually serranos or jalapenos), olive oil, and salt. It is popular across the Levant, but especially in Jordan and Palestine on account of its easy preparation and healthy ingredients."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-qalayet-bandora"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qalayet_bandora"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Jordanian cuisine","cuisine/jordanian-cuisine"],["Levantine cuisine","cuisine/levantine-cuisine"],["Palestinian cuisine","cuisine/palestinian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Tomatoes","ingredient/tomatoes"]]},{"h":"More from Jordanian cuisine","links":[["Qatayef","dish/qatayef"],["Stuffed squash","dish/stuffed-squash"],["Taboon bread","dish/taboon-bread"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/54/%D9%82%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%8A%D8%A9_%D8%A8%D9%86%D8%AF%D9%88%D8%B1%D8%A9.jpg/500px-%D9%82%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%8A%D8%A9_%D8%A8%D9%86%D8%AF%D9%88%D8%B1%D8%A9.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Rania al-Bahara","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%82%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%8A%D8%A9_%D8%A8%D9%86%D8%AF%D9%88%D8%B1%D8%A9.jpg"}},"dish/suan-la-fen":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/suan-la-fen","n":"Suan la fen","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Sichuan cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Hot and sour noodles is a dish which comes from Sichuan, China and is a popular part of Sichuan cuisine. The noodles are made from starch derived from peas, potato, sweet potato, or rice."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-suan-la-fen"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suan_la_fen"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Sichuan cuisine","cuisine/sichuan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Chili oil","ingredient/chili-oil"],["Kuanfen noodles","ingredient/kuanfen-noodles"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"China Sichuan Food","ref":"site/chinasichuanfood-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"chinasichuanfood.com","note":"Elaine's blog of Chinese home recipes with a strong Sichuan focus.","url":"https://www.chinasichuanfood.com/","suggested":true},{"name":"The Mala Market Blog","ref":"site/blog-themalamarket-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"blog.themalamarket.com","note":"Recipes and ingredient guides from a US importer of Sichuan pantry staples.","url":"https://blog.themalamarket.com/mala-market-blog-about-page/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Sichuan cuisine","links":[["Doubanjiang","dish/doubanjiang"],["Fish with pickled mustard greens","dish/fish-with-pickled-mustard-greens"],["Kuanfen noodles","dish/kuanfen-noodles"],["Laziji","dish/laziji"],["Yuxiang shredded pork","dish/yuxiang-shredded-pork"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/88/Hot_and_sour_noodles_with_pork_intestines.jpg/500px-Hot_and_sour_noodles_with_pork_intestines.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Jpatokal","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hot_and_sour_noodles_with_pork_intestines.jpg"}},"dish/the-oasis-on-lake-travis":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/the-oasis-on-lake-travis","n":"The Oasis on Lake Travis","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["The Oasis on Lake Tra